Z||000000||penetrator||07-18-2000||10:26 AM||||Does anyone have any good idea how i should seal my leak between the cat and the "end" exhaust. The problem is that my new exhaust have a bigger (like 5-7 millimeters) radius than the cat pipe, so i cant get it sealed. And i tried the common "leak-fixing kits" but know the leak is back again and the car runs bad and getting backfires. Can i use some of this "NASA liquid metal thing", or cant it stand the heat? I have been thinking of to put in a piece of pipe between them, but then i must remove the exhaust system again.<P>Any good tips that will work on my case?<BR>(Hope you understand my bad english)<BR> ||||reg||10
Z||000001||Monkeyman||07-18-2000||12:21 PM||||I don't have any idea what you could do to fix it, but I wouldn't worry too much about your "bad english".  I know plenty of Americans who can't speak that well.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fret Naught||07-18-2000||12:33 PM||||I have had pretty good luck in the past with muffler tape, you wrap it around the joint, (under the clamp) then when it heats up it turns to a gooy globy mess, but it does seal and stands up to the heat real good.||||reg||7
Z||000003||penetrator||07-19-2000||06:45 AM||||Fret Naught:<BR>Ive allready tried with tape, it worked for a month, but the i was "racing" with my friends Ferarri 348tb on a smaller road and was using the 3rd gear all the time, and when i was motorbraking into some curves the tape could't stand the pressure and flaked up. So now it sounds like a topfuel again. Cool but the engine is jumpy and running pour. Thanks.<P>Monkeyman:<BR>Well, thats a good thing Or is it a bad thing =)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||07-19-2000||10:44 AM||||Why not have it welded, seems that would be the most reliable and permenant solution.....<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
